;; DRIVER-LL sub-phase 4e: the serial wave, stage M.
;;
;; This is the module that gives `fill.fill-accepted`, `fill.next-error-budget`,
;; `fill.is-finding` and `token.token-during` their FIRST CALLER. Before it, all
;; four were proved, carried four refute cruxes between them, and decided
;; nothing: `scripts/tests/test_driver_ll_callers.py` asserted twelve such defs
;; by name. A proof about a function no program calls is still a valid proof,
;; which is exactly why no gate could catch it, and why stage H's own defect
;; turned on our own artifacts is what this sub-phase exists to close.
;;
;; A LIBRARY SINCE PROGRAM UNIFICATION JOB (a2), AND IT WAS A SEPARATE PROGRAM
;; BEFORE. The paragraph here used to argue that stage M could not be a
;; sequencer arm, because the sequencer's `Ctl` carries one `Delegate Body` arm
;; per stage and stage M is holes TIMES attempts. The fold answered that with a
;; `Fan` arm carrying this module's whole state, so the argument is history and
;; is kept only so a reader who finds it quoted elsewhere knows it was retired.
;; This module has no `def-main`; `sequencer wave ...` reaches the same machine.
;;
;; WHAT STAGE M OWES ITS AGENT, which sub-phase 4e did not port and the stage M
;; agent contract adds. 4e settled that the checkout brief is the agent's only
;; input and called that the sole-channel discipline. driver-spec section 8 does
;; not say that, and the Phase 4 proposal's own section 5 item 1 refuted it
;; first: that item settled `--agent-exe` plus repeatable `--agent-arg`, with
;; the placeholders SUBSTITUTED PER ARGUMENT, and 4e built a stage that
;; substitutes nothing and appends two paths instead. Section 8 part 3 settles
;; the term by definition: the driver MUST be able to report any file in an
;; agent's directory that was not among ITS DECLARED INPUTS, so a declared input
;; is a driver-side commitment and a rendered task statement is one. The four
;; declared inputs are the rendered prompt, the checkout brief, the pristine
;; subject copy of section 8 part 4, and the language reference.
;;
;; THE FOUR ABSTRACTION FUNCTIONS ARE THE UNPROVED SEAM, and §7 of the proposal
;; inventories them as such. They are shell-side observations of another
;; process's output, so they are outside Sigma_auto and are not Lean candidates
;; either: there is no proposition for a prover to discharge about what a string
;; from someone else's stdout means. What the proved cores decide is what
;; FOLLOWS from those booleans; what nothing checks is that the booleans are
;; read correctly. Each is written once, here, so the seam is auditable at one
;; site rather than spread across the state machine.

(module wave
  (import wasi.io   (capability stdout :deterministic false))
  (import wasi.fs   (capability read-write "/"))
  (import wasi.proc (capability exec :deterministic false))

  (import fill)  (open fill)
  (import token) (open token)
  (import common)   (open common)

  ;; ---- the shell-side abstraction functions -------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; MEASURED against compiler v0.14.87 by the 4e harness leg rather than read
  ;; off the spec, because two of the four were described wrongly in the
  ;; proposal until Rev 15.

  ;; `llmll patch` emits JSON on STDOUT with a five-way `result` discriminator
  ;; (PatchApply.hs's ToJSON instance); stderr was EMPTY on every invocation
  ;; measured. So this is a test on a structured field and not, as §7 said
  ;; until Rev 15, a substring test over stderr prose.
  (def-shell patch-succeeded? [out: string] -> bool
    (string-contains out "PatchSuccess"))

  ;; CONTENTION IS NARROWER THAN PatchAuthError. `invalid or expired checkout
  ;; token` carries the same constructor and is NOT contention; only the three
  ;; `obligation context is stale ...` messages are. The reference's own
  ;; predicate retries on either token, which charges a non-contention auth
  ;; failure to the budget [S9-SEPARATE] says never depletes, and the port must
  ;; not inherit it.
  ;;
  ;; Keyed on the ASCII lexeme `stale` and NEVER on the fuller phrase: the
  ;; compiler's message carries U+2014 EM DASH immediately after it, which is
  ;; FS-ENCODING-1 territory. The `result` test is what keeps the lexeme from
  ;; matching a diagnostic that merely mentions staleness.
  (def-shell contention? [out: string] -> bool
    (and (string-contains out "PatchAuthError")
         (string-contains out "stale")))

  ;; DUPLICATED WITH sequencer.llmll ON PURPOSE, and program unification does
  ;; NOT retire it, but it IS renamed. The body is identical in both, so a
  ;; body-equality census
  ;; puts it in the collapse set; the census is wrong here, because the two
  ;; copies are pinned by two different covers for two different reasons. This
  ;; copy is a member of SEAM in test_driver_ll_4e.py: the wave's functions that
  ;; read another process's stdout and prove nothing about it. The sequencer's
  ;; copy is pinned by test_driver_ll_4d.py as an abstraction function over the
  ;; compiler's transcript. Collapsing it into common.llmll erases both pins and
  ;; trades a verification-disclosure claim for eight saved lines. Moving it was
  ;; TRIED at the unification job (a) pass and both covers failed, which is the
  ;; covers working.
  (def-shell wave-verify-safe? [out: string] -> bool
    (string-contains out "SAFE"))

  ;; DISCLOSURE, and it is sharper than "unproved". Rev 15 measured that this
  ;; boolean does NOT mean what [S9-FAITHFUL] says it means. A body whose only
  ;; callee is an UNFILLED hole appears in the body-faithful set, because the
  ;; line reports that the function's VC was EMITTED body-faithfully, which is
  ;; compatible with every callee's contract having been assumed. So per-fill
  ;; acceptance is PROVISIONAL; the closing check is the whole-tree
  ;; --strict-verified-core after every hole is filled. `crux-fill-accepts-
  ;; assumed` does not catch this, a refute crux perturbing the proved decision
  ;; and not the mapping into it.
  ;; `target` and not `fn`: `fn` is the lambda keyword and is reserved.
  (def-shell body-faithful? [out: string target: string] -> bool
    (and (string-contains out "body-faithful:")
         (string-contains out target)))

  ;; NO PRODUCER EXISTS for the fourth conjunct. The reference's `_verify_fn`
  ;; computes safe, body_faithful and refuted and NOTHING for termination,
  ;; despite its own docstring naming `termination_unverified` in the bar. It
  ;; is passed as a literal here and that is a gap, recorded rather than
  ;; papered over: passing `true` would make [S9-ACCEPT]'s fourth conjunct
  ;; vacuous, and passing `false` would reject every fill.
  (def-shell termination-observed? [out: string] -> bool
    (not (string-contains out "termination_unverified")))

  ;; THE CLOSING CHECK, and it is the one that is not provisional. Per-fill
  ;; acceptance cannot see whether a callee's contract was assumed; the
  ;; whole-tree `--strict-verified-core` can, and it is what Q-005 leaves as
  ;; the residue of the [S9-FAITHFUL] measurement above.
  ;;
  ;; TWO CONJUNCTS BECAUSE THE FAILURE IS SILENT ON ONE. Measured at v0.14.87
  ;; over this module's own fixture: a tree with holes left in it prints
  ;; `ERROR: --strict-verified-core: N function(s) fell back from
  ;; body-faithful verification` and NO `SAFE` line, and a fully filled tree
  ;; prints `body-faithful:` for every function and then SAFE. Keying on SAFE
  ;; alone would pass a run that never reached the solver at all.
  (def-shell seal-holds? [out: string] -> bool
    (and (string-contains out "SAFE")
         (not (string-contains out "fell back"))))

  ;; ---- the proved-core call sites -----------------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; Four call sites, one per previously-callerless def. Each is a one-line
  ;; forward so the call is visible to a reader and to the callerless census,
  ;; rather than buried inside a state-machine arm.

  ;; fill.fill-accepted. The per-fill bar of driver-spec section 9.
  (def-shell accept-fill? [applied: bool out: string target: string] -> bool
    (fill-accepted applied
                   (wave-verify-safe? out)
                   (body-faithful? out target)
                   (termination-observed? out)))

  ;; fill.next-error-budget. THE SEPARATED BUDGETS. Contention leaves the error
  ;; budget alone; a wrong body spends it.
  (def-shell spend-budget [err: int out: string] -> int
    (next-error-budget err (contention? out)))

  ;; fill.is-finding. Only an exhausted error budget yields a finding, so a
  ;; checkout fault or an agent timeout is reported as itself and never as a
  ;; hole the agent could not fill.
  (def-shell finding? [err-remaining: int protocol-exhausted: bool] -> bool
    (is-finding err-remaining protocol-exhausted))

  ;; token.token-during. The linear checkout protocol, phase-indexed.
  ;;
  ;; §10:371-373's release-then-obtain-fresh ORDERING is a SHELL OBLIGATION and
  ;; not something the core proves: token-during is a memoryless function of
  ;; the current phase, so it constrains each step and says nothing about the
  ;; sequence of steps. The ordering is discharged HERE, by releasing before
  ;; the agent call and taking a fresh checkout at submission, and by nothing
  ;; in token.llmll.
  (def-shell token-state-now [p: Phase] -> TokenState
    (token-during p))

  ;; The two obligations that ordering places on this module, stated as
  ;; predicates over the phase so a reader can check the state machine against
  ;; them rather than against an intention.
  (def-shell must-release-before-agent? [p: Phase] -> bool
    (match (token-state-now p)
      ((Released) true)
      ((Held)     false)))

  (def-shell holds-token? [p: Phase] -> bool
    (match (token-state-now p)
      ((Released) false)
      ((Held)     true)))

  ;; ==========================================================================
  ;; THE WAVE ITSELF
  ;; ==========================================================================
  ;;
  ;; Everything above decides; everything below performs. The split is the same
  ;; one spine.llmll and sequencer.llmll keep, and it is why this module is the
  ;; asserted tier throughout: every def here is `def-shell`, and the four
  ;; proved decisions it consumes live in fill.llmll and token.llmll, which are
  ;; `--strict-verified-core` clean.
  ;;
  ;; THE PROTOCOL, one hole at a time, driver-spec.txt section 9 and section 10:
  ;;
  ;;   checkout -> release BEFORE the agent call -> agent -> fresh checkout ->
  ;;   patch -> verify -> accept or revert
  ;;
  ;; The release in position two is the whole point. crux-token-held-across-call
  ;; is the shipped defect it prevents: a token held across the agent call
  ;; wedged fourteen holes and lost one fill on the first RFC-SWARM wave.
  ;;
  ;; MEASURED AGAINST v0.14.87, because none of it is in the spec. The CLI facts
  ;; this machine is built on were established by running the compiler over this
  ;; module's own fixture tree rather than read off a document:
  ;;
  ;;   * `checkout` and `patch` take a .ast.json, NOT a .llmll. `llmll build
  ;;     FILE --emit` writes it to generated/<name>/<name>.ast.json. `--emit` is
  ;;     a bare flag; `--emit json-ast` is a usage error.
  ;;   * `holes AST --json` puts an array on stdout, each entry carrying
  ;;     `pointer` and `module-path` (the latter as "def add-one", so the
  ;;     function name is its last space-separated field).
  ;;   * the brief's token field is `token`; the brief also carries `source_hash`
  ;;     and `ttl`. There is no `hole_node` field, which is why the patch below
  ;;     is a BARE REPLACE and carries no `test` op: the reference injects the
  ;;     node itself after reading it out of the tree, and a test op would cost
  ;;     this module an RFC 6901 pointer walk over the AST for an assertion the
  ;;     CAS already makes. `parsePatchOp` accepts a patch list of one replace,
  ;;     measured: PatchSuccess.
  ;;   * `patch` REWRITES THE TREE IN PLACE, so a rejected fill needs an undo.
  ;;     The backup is taken per attempt, immediately before the patch, so a
  ;;     restore returns the tree to the state that attempt found and never
  ;;     discards a sibling hole's accepted fill.
  ;;   * A STALE PATCH LEAVES THE LOCK HELD. After a PatchAuthError the hole
  ;;     cannot be checked out again ("hole at ... is already checked out")
  ;;     until the stale token is released. That is the same wedge
  ;;     crux-token-held-across-call is about, reached by a different route, and
  ;;     it is why the contention path releases before it retries rather than
  ;;     looping straight back to a checkout.

  ;; ---- the run record ------------------------------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; Proposal section 13 risk 6: a wrong projection over a pair chain
  ;; TYPECHECKS. Every projection in this module is in one of the accessors
  ;; below and no use site writes a bare (first (second ...)).

  ;; THE DECLARED INPUTS, driver-spec section 8 part 3, as one record rather
  ;; than three more slots on WaveCfg. Three slots and a maximum projection
  ;; depth of two, which is the point: WaveCfg's chain is already eight deep and
  ;; risk 6 of the Phase 4 proposal is that a wrong projection over a pair chain
  ;; TYPECHECKS. A twelve-deep chain of mostly-strings is how that defect gets
  ;; written, so the three paths travel together under their own accessors.
  ;;
  ;; tpl-path, (pristine, refdir)
  ;;
  ;;   tpl-path  the stage-M-fill.md template, resolved. The sequencer builds it
  ;;             from --prompts-dir and registry.stage-prompt; the standalone
  ;;             `wave` sub-command takes it as --prompt.
  ;;   pristine  the ORIGINAL, unmodified subject, which is the authored
  ;;             roots.llmll and never the .ast.json this wave patches in place.
  ;;   refdir    the language reference's root, or "" for no copies. The
  ;;             sequencer passes "" when registry.stage-provision-ref? is false,
  ;;             so the decision stays in the table.
  (type Decl (| Decl (string, (string, string))))

  ;; tree, (workdir, (llmll, (agent, (agent-args, (timeout, (err0, (proto0,
  ;; decl)))))))
  (type WaveCfg (| WaveCfg (string, (string, (string, (string, (list[string], (int, (int, (int, Decl))))))))))

  ;; One attempt at one hole: k, (n, (err, (proto, (token, (body, errors))))).
  ;; The token and the body node are per-attempt and are cleared by at-next, so
  ;; a retry cannot resubmit the previous attempt's token against a re-read
  ;; tree.
  ;;
  ;; `errors` IS THE SEVENTH SLOT AND IT IS NOT CLEARED, which is the opposite
  ;; of the two before it. It carries the PREVIOUS attempt's compiler output
  ;; into the next attempt's prompt, and the reference renders it as {{errors}}.
  ;; Without it attempt n+1 receives byte-identical input to attempt n, which
  ;; makes the error budget a sampling loop where driver-spec section 9 means it
  ;; as a repair loop. `fill.next-error-budget` is unaffected and was always
  ;; correct; what changes is whether the running program supplies the input
  ;; that gives the budget meaning.
  ;;
  ;; THREE STRINGS IN A ROW IS THE HAZARD THIS COMMENT EXISTS FOR. `token`,
  ;; `body` and `errors` all project to `string`, so a swapped accessor
  ;; typechecks and passes every type-level gate. The three accessors are
  ;; written once, below, and `test_driver_ll_stage_m.py` asserts they project
  ;; three distinct positions.
  (type Att (| Att (int, (int, (int, (int, (string, (string, string))))))))

  ;; cfg, (holes, (accepted, (findings, (protocol-failures, fills)))). The holes
  ;; travel as raw list[Json] straight from `llmll holes --json`, on
  ;; sequencer.llmll's precedent for the manifest: a custom carrier would have to
  ;; be re-derived from the same fields at every use and would add a decoding
  ;; step that can disagree with the compiler.
  ;;
  ;; `fills` IS A SIXTH SLOT AND THE THREE COUNTERS DO NOT SUBSUME IT. The
  ;; counters answer "how many"; `12-wave/wave.json` declares a row PER HOLE
  ;; carrying which hole, which pointer, and how many attempts it took. Stage M
  ;; declares that file (registry.llmll, stage-out i=13 j=0) and this module did
  ;; not write it until now, so one of stage M's two declared outputs did not
  ;; exist. The clause 2 comparator reads `fills` for R5 and `attempts` for R6.
  ;; `tpl` IS A SEVENTH SLOT AND IT IS RUN STATE, NOT CONFIG. The template text
  ;; is known only after the boot read, so putting it in WaveCfg would mean
  ;; rebuilding a record whose whole point is that it does not change. It sits
  ;; beside `fills`, which is `Json`, so a swapped projection between the two is
  ;; a type error rather than a silent defect.
  (type Wv (| Wv (WaveCfg, (list[Json], (int, (int, (int, (Json, string))))))))

  ;; Each arm names the RESPONSE it is about to receive, since the console loop
  ;; delivers the response to the command issued on entry to the arm. Named
  ;; WCtl and not Phase or Ctl, and that is not cosmetic: `Phase` is token's
  ;; nullary sum, opened above, and the type checker CONFLATES same-named sum
  ;; types across modules (measured at v0.14.87, only an open-shadow-warning
  ;; fires). Naming this one Phase would let a payload-carrying arm satisfy an
  ;; annotation resolved to token's Phase. The sequencer's Phase was renamed to
  ;; Ctl for the same reason.
  ;;
  ;;   WBoot 0     r is the argument vector
  ;;   WBoot 1     r is the `llmll holes --json` output
  ;;   Brief      r is the FIRST checkout brief, whose token is released at once
  ;;   Rel        r is that release
  ;;   Working    r is the agent's body artifact, read after the agent exits
  ;;   Fresh      r is the SECOND checkout brief, taken at submission
  ;;   Applied    r is the `llmll patch` result JSON
  ;;   Checked    r is the `llmll verify` transcript
  ;;   Closing    r is the release that follows an accepted fill
  ;;   Undoing    r is the release that follows a restore
  ;;   Sealing    r is the whole-tree --strict-verified-core transcript, and
  ;;              its int payload is the code the per-hole tally alone earned
  (type WCtl
    (| WBoot    int)
    (| Brief   Att)
    (| Rel     Att)
    (| Working Att)
    (| Fresh   Att)
    (| Applied Att)
    (| Checked Att)
    (| Closing Att)
    (| Undoing Att)
    (| Sealing int)
    (| WEnding  int)
    (| WDone    int))

  ;; ---- accessors, written once ---------------------------------------------

  (def-shell wcfg-tree [c: WaveCfg] -> string
    (match c ((WaveCfg p) (first p))))
  (def-shell wcfg-workdir [c: WaveCfg] -> string
    (match c ((WaveCfg p) (first (second p)))))
  (def-shell wcfg-compiler [c: WaveCfg] -> string
    (match c ((WaveCfg p) (first (second (second p))))))
  (def-shell wcfg-agent [c: WaveCfg] -> string
    (match c ((WaveCfg p) (first (second (second (second p)))))))
  (def-shell wcfg-agent-args [c: WaveCfg] -> list[string]
    (match c ((WaveCfg p) (first (second (second (second (second p))))))))
  (def-shell wcfg-timeout [c: WaveCfg] -> int
    (match c ((WaveCfg p) (first (second (second (second (second (second p)))))))))
  (def-shell wcfg-err0 [c: WaveCfg] -> int
    (match c ((WaveCfg p) (first (second (second (second (second (second (second p))))))))))
  (def-shell wcfg-proto0 [c: WaveCfg] -> int
    (match c ((WaveCfg p) (first (second (second (second (second (second (second (second p)))))))))))
  (def-shell wcfg-decl [c: WaveCfg] -> Decl
    (match c ((WaveCfg p) (second (second (second (second (second (second (second (second p)))))))))))

  ;; The declared inputs, projected at depth two at most. `mk-decl` is the one
  ;; constructor and `sequencer.llmll`'s `fan-cfg` is its second caller.
  (def-shell mk-decl [tpl: string pristine: string refdir: string] -> Decl
    (Decl (pair tpl (pair pristine refdir))))
  (def-shell decl-tpl-path [d: Decl] -> string
    (match d ((Decl p) (first p))))
  (def-shell decl-pristine [d: Decl] -> string
    (match d ((Decl p) (first (second p)))))
  (def-shell decl-refdir [d: Decl] -> string
    (match d ((Decl p) (second (second p)))))

  (def-shell wcfg-tpl-path [c: WaveCfg] -> string
    (decl-tpl-path (wcfg-decl c)))
  (def-shell wcfg-pristine [c: WaveCfg] -> string
    (decl-pristine (wcfg-decl c)))
  (def-shell wcfg-refdir [c: WaveCfg] -> string
    (decl-refdir (wcfg-decl c)))

  (def-shell at-k [a: Att] -> int
    (match a ((Att q) (first q))))
  (def-shell at-n [a: Att] -> int
    (match a ((Att q) (first (second q)))))
  (def-shell at-err [a: Att] -> int
    (match a ((Att q) (first (second (second q))))))
  (def-shell at-proto [a: Att] -> int
    (match a ((Att q) (first (second (second (second q)))))))
  (def-shell at-token [a: Att] -> string
    (match a ((Att q) (first (second (second (second (second q))))))))
  (def-shell at-body [a: Att] -> string
    (match a ((Att q) (first (second (second (second (second (second q)))))))))
  (def-shell at-errors [a: Att] -> string
    (match a ((Att q) (second (second (second (second (second (second q)))))))))

  (def-shell mk-att [k: int n: int err: int proto: int
                     tok: string body: string errs: string] -> Att
    (Att (pair k (pair n (pair err (pair proto (pair tok (pair body errs))))))))

  (def-shell at-with-token [a: Att t: string] -> Att
    (mk-att (at-k a) (at-n a) (at-err a) (at-proto a) t (at-body a) (at-errors a)))
  (def-shell at-with-body [a: Att b: string] -> Att
    (mk-att (at-k a) (at-n a) (at-err a) (at-proto a) (at-token a) b (at-errors a)))

  ;; The NEXT attempt at the same hole, carrying the budgets its caller
  ;; computed and dropping the token and the body. Bumping `n` is what gives
  ;; every attempt its own directory, so no arm can read a previous attempt's
  ;; stdout file and mistake it for its own.
  ;;
  ;; `errs` IS A PARAMETER AND NOT A CLEAR, which is why the caller states it.
  ;; The two checkout failures carry the previous text forward, because a
  ;; checkout that produced no token says nothing about the fill and the
  ;; reference does not overwrite `errors` there either. The agent-failure and
  ;; rejection paths supply new text. A cleared slot would put the next attempt
  ;; back on byte-identical input, which is the defect this slot exists to fix,
  ;; so no default is offered here.
  (def-shell at-next [a: Att err: int proto: int errs: string] -> Att
    (mk-att (at-k a) (+ (at-n a) 1) err proto "" "" errs))

  (def-shell w-cfg [w: Wv] -> WaveCfg
    (match w ((Wv p) (first p))))
  (def-shell w-holes [w: Wv] -> list[Json]
    (match w ((Wv p) (first (second p)))))
  (def-shell w-ok [w: Wv] -> int
    (match w ((Wv p) (first (second (second p))))))
  (def-shell w-find [w: Wv] -> int
    (match w ((Wv p) (first (second (second (second p)))))))
  (def-shell w-bad [w: Wv] -> int
    (match w ((Wv p) (first (second (second (second (second p))))))))
  (def-shell w-fills [w: Wv] -> Json
    (match w ((Wv p) (first (second (second (second (second (second p)))))))))
  (def-shell w-tpl [w: Wv] -> string
    (match w ((Wv p) (second (second (second (second (second (second p)))))))))

  (def-shell mk-wv [c: WaveCfg hs: list[Json] ok: int fnd: int bad: int
                    fl: Json tpl: string] -> Wv
    (Wv (pair c (pair hs (pair ok (pair fnd (pair bad (pair fl tpl))))))))

  (def-shell w-with-tpl [w: Wv t: string] -> Wv
    (mk-wv (w-cfg w) (w-holes w) (w-ok w) (w-find w) (w-bad w) (w-fills w) t))

  (def-shell w-fills-list [w: Wv] -> list[Json]
    (unwrap-or (json-array (w-fills w)) (list-empty)))

  (def-shell w-of [s: (Wv, WCtl)] -> Wv (first s))
  (def-shell ctl-of [s: (Wv, WCtl)] -> WCtl (second s))
  (def-shell wave-go [w: Wv p: WCtl c: Command] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (pair (pair w p) c))

  ;; ---- responses, argv and small helpers -----------------------------------

  (def-shell wave-text-of [r: Response] -> string
    (match r
      ((RText t) t)
      ((RErr _)  "")
      ((RNone)   "")
      ((RCode _) "")
      ((RList _) "")))

  (def-shell clamp0 [n: int] -> int (if (< n 0) 0 n))

  ;; ONE CONSTRUCTOR, and it exists because there are now two callers. The
  ;; standalone `wave` sub-command parses argv; `sequencer.llmll`'s stage M arm
  ;; builds the same record out of its own `Cfg` and the registry. A second
  ;; hand-written `WaveCfg (pair ...)` chain would be a wrong-projection defect
  ;; waiting to happen, which is what the accessors above already exist to stop.
  (def-shell mk-wave-cfg [tree: string workdir: string llmll: string
                          agent: string agent-args: list[string]
                          timeout: int err0: int proto0: int
                          decl: Decl] -> WaveCfg
    (WaveCfg (pair tree
               (pair workdir
                     (pair llmll
                           (pair agent
                                 (pair agent-args
                                       (pair timeout
                                             (pair err0
                                                   (pair proto0 decl))))))))))

  ;; THE STANDALONE SUB-COMMAND TAKES PATHS AND THE STAGE ARM COMPOSES THEM.
  ;; `--prompt` is the resolved template and not a directory, because this
  ;; module does not import the registry and so cannot turn --prompts-dir plus
  ;; a stage index into a file name. `fan-cfg` in sequencer.llmll does exactly
  ;; that and hands the result over, which keeps the registry the one table that
  ;; knows a stage's prompt basename.
  (def-shell parse-wave-cfg [as: list[string]] -> WaveCfg
    (mk-wave-cfg (flag-value as "--tree")
                 (dflt (flag-value as "--workdir") ".wave")
                 (flag-value as "--llmll-cmd")
                 (flag-value as "--agent-cmd")
                 (flag-values as "--agent-arg")
                 (int-flag as "--timeout" 900)
                 (int-flag as "--error-budget" 3)
                 (int-flag as "--protocol-budget" 3)
                 (mk-decl (flag-value as "--prompt")
                          (flag-value as "--pristine")
                          (flag-value as "--reference-dir"))))

  ;; "" when the run is startable.
  ;;
  ;; Both budgets must be POSITIVE and not merely non-negative:
  ;; `next-error-budget` carries `pre (>= err 0)` and every call site below
  ;; spends before it re-tests, so a zero-valued budget would reach that
  ;; precondition with -1. The check belongs here, once, rather than as a clamp
  ;; at four call sites.
  ;;
  ;; THE .ast.json CHECK IS NOT PEDANTRY AND THE COVER'S W6 IS WHY IT EXISTS.
  ;; `holes` accepts a .llmll and answers the real hole list, so a wave pointed
  ;; at source enumerates two holes and looks healthy; `checkout` then refuses
  ;; every one of them, and the run burns both budgets to reach two protocol
  ;; failures and exit 3. The first version of this module claimed instead that
  ;; a .llmll "reads as zero holes", which is false, and the cover cell written
  ;; to that claim is what caught it. Stopping at parse turns an eight-step
  ;; misdiagnosis into one line.
  ;;
  ;; THE TWO NEW FLAGS COME AFTER --agent-cmd AND THE ORDER IS NOT COSMETIC.
  ;; Cover cell W5 drops --agent-cmd alone and asserts the message names that
  ;; flag. A check placed above it would answer a different flag for the same
  ;; input and the cell would grade a message no operator would see.
  (def-shell wave-missing-flags [c: WaveCfg] -> string
    (if (string-empty? (wcfg-tree c))
        "--tree is required (the .ast.json the wave fills; llmll build FILE --emit writes it)"
      (if (not (string-contains (wcfg-tree c) ".ast.json"))
        "--tree must be a .ast.json; checkout and patch refuse .llmll source (llmll build FILE --emit writes generated/<name>/<name>.ast.json, and --emit is a bare flag)"
        (if (string-empty? (wcfg-compiler c))
            "--llmll-cmd is required (the compiler this wave shells out to)"
            (if (string-empty? (wcfg-agent c))
                "--agent-cmd is required (the executable that authors one body node)"
                (if (string-empty? (wcfg-tpl-path c))
                    "--prompt is required (the stage-M-fill.md template this wave renders per attempt; driver-spec sec 8 makes the rendered task statement a declared input)"
                    (if (string-empty? (wcfg-pristine c))
                        "--pristine is required (the ORIGINAL roots.llmll the agent self-checks against; driver-spec sec 8 part 4 forbids handing over the tree this wave patches)"
                        (if (< (wcfg-err0 c) 1)
                            "--error-budget must be at least 1"
                            (if (< (wcfg-proto0 c) 1)
                                "--protocol-budget must be at least 1"
                                "")))))))))

  ;; ---- paths ---------------------------------------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; One directory per (hole, attempt). Not tidiness: every `llmll` invocation
  ;; below is folded into ONE arm as `run then read`, so the arm never sees the
  ;; process exit status and reads the stdout FILE instead. A per-attempt path
  ;; is what makes an absent file distinguishable from a stale one, and a
  ;; failed checkout is detected by exactly that -- its stdout is empty, so the
  ;; token parses as "".

  (def-shell att-dir [c: WaveCfg a: Att] -> string
    (string-concat-many [(wcfg-workdir c) "/h" (int-to-string (at-k a))
                         "-a" (int-to-string (at-n a))]))

  (def-shell in-dir [c: WaveCfg a: Att name: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many [(att-dir c a) "/" name]))

  (def-shell llmll-run [c: WaveCfg a: Att args: list[string] out: string err: string] -> Command
    (wasi.proc.run (wcfg-compiler c) args "." (in-dir c a out) (in-dir c a err)
                   (wcfg-timeout c) "/dev/null"))

  ;; ---- the declared inputs -------------------------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; driver-spec section 8, in the three parts that reach this module. Part 2:
  ;; the agent's directory holds only its declared inputs. Part 3: the driver
  ;; must be able to report a file that was not among them. Part 4: the
  ;; self-check copy MUST be the original, unmodified subject.
  ;;
  ;; PART 3 IS NOT IMPLEMENTED AND IS `FS-ISOLATION-1`. This module provisions
  ;; the declared set and audits nothing, which is the reference's posture
  ;; exactly. `wasi.proc.run` takes an executable, an argument vector, a working
  ;; directory, three handle paths and a timeout, and NO confinement parameter,
  ;; so a confining spawn is a language change rather than a port decision; it
  ;; is recorded as Q-008. Two things in this workdir are reachable by an agent
  ;; that goes looking: the live tree named by --tree, and `tree.bak`, which
  ;; `undo` writes into an attempt directory and which carries every sibling
  ;; hole's accepted fill. The pristine copy removes the MOTIVE for looking and
  ;; the prompt instructs against it. Neither is a structural bar.

  ;; The prompt's file name, in the agent's own directory. `PROMPT.md` is what
  ;; `prompt-file` in sequencer.llmll writes for every other delegated stage.
  (def-shell prompt-path [c: WaveCfg a: Att] -> string
    (in-dir c a "PROMPT.md"))

  ;; `scratch.llmll` is the reference's name for it and the template names that
  ;; file to the agent, so this string and the template agree or the agent is
  ;; told to check work against a file that is not there.
  (def-shell scratch-path [c: WaveCfg a: Att] -> string
    (in-dir c a "scratch.llmll"))

  ;; The language reference: LLMLL.md and the JSON-AST schema, which is
  ;; `_provision_reference`'s pair. The tool manual and not the answer; neither
  ;; file says anything about the target RFC.
  ;;
  ;; THE SCHEMA LOSES ITS DIRECTORY ON THE WAY IN, as the reference's
  ;; `wd / src.name` does: the agent's copy is `llmll-ast.schema.json` at the
  ;; top of its own directory, which is the name the template tells it to read.
  (def-shell ref-copies-cmd [c: WaveCfg a: Att tail: Command] -> Command
    (if (string-empty? (wcfg-refdir c))
        tail
        (seq-commands
          (wasi.fs.copy (string-concat (wcfg-refdir c) "/LLMLL.md")
                        (in-dir c a "LLMLL.md"))
          (seq-commands
            (wasi.fs.copy (string-concat (wcfg-refdir c) "/docs/llmll-ast.schema.json")
                          (in-dir c a "llmll-ast.schema.json"))
            tail))))

  ;; PER ATTEMPT AND NOT PER HOLE, because the directory is per attempt. The
  ;; reference provisions once per hole, outside its retry loop, since its agent
  ;; directory is per hole. Following the directory is the safe direction: each
  ;; attempt's directory then holds its own declared inputs rather than
  ;; inheriting them from an attempt that has already been judged.
  (def-shell provision-cmd [c: WaveCfg a: Att tail: Command] -> Command
    (seq-commands
      (wasi.fs.copy (wcfg-pristine c) (scratch-path c a))
      (ref-copies-cmd c a tail)))

  ;; ---- the rendered task statement -----------------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; THE WAVE RENDERS AND THE STAGE LOOP DOES NOT, and that is a design decision
  ;; rather than a convenience. Every placeholder in `registry.stage-pre-key`
  ;; names an artifact read ONCE per stage before delegating, which is why
  ;; `render` in sequencer.llmll can run in the stage loop. Three of stage M's
  ;; four are settled per attempt inside the hole loop: `{{hole}}` is the
  ;; function name, `{{brief}}` is the checkout brief re-taken each attempt, and
  ;; `{{errors}}` is the previous attempt's compiler output. `{{llmll}}` is the
  ;; exception and is substituted here too, so one function holds the whole set.
  ;;
  ;; `registry.stage-pre-count` for stage M is therefore ZERO and stays zero:
  ;; stage M reads no artifact BEFORE delegating.
  ;;
  ;; THE FIRST-ATTEMPT LITERAL IS THE REFERENCE'S, `errors or "(first attempt)"`.
  ;; Rendering an empty string would leave the prompt's last-but-one heading
  ;; with nothing under it, which reads to an agent as an attempt that produced
  ;; no output rather than as an attempt that has not happened.
  (def-shell errors-or-first [errs: string] -> string
    (if (string-empty? errs) "(first attempt)" errs))

  (def-shell render-prompt [tpl: string hole: string llmll: string
                            brief: string errs: string] -> string
    (replace-all
      (replace-all
        (replace-all (replace-all tpl "{{hole}}" hole) "{{llmll}}" llmll)
        "{{errors}}" (errors-or-first errs))
      "{{brief}}" brief))

  ;; `rendered` AND NOT `out`, AND THE PARAMETER NAME IS THE WHOLE POINT: the
  ;; seam census in test_driver_ll_4e.py identifies a reader of another
  ;; process's stdout as a function taking `out: string` that calls
  ;; `string-contains`. A
  ;; parameter named `out` here would file a check on the driver's OWN prompt
  ;; into the unproved-transcript seam, where it does not belong. The census
  ;; names this function in NOT_A_SUBPROCESS_READ instead, with that reason.
  ;;
  ;; The reference's `ctx.prompt` ends with `require(not left, ...)`, so an
  ;; unfilled placeholder is a stage failure there and is one here.
  (def-shell has-unfilled? [rendered: string] -> bool
    (string-contains rendered "{{"))

  ;; ---- reading what the compiler said --------------------------------------

  (def-shell holes-in [s: string] -> list[Json]
    (match (json-parse s)
      ((Success v) (unwrap-or (json-array v) (list-empty)))
      ((Error _)   (list-empty))))

  (def-shell token-in [s: string] -> string
    (match (json-parse s)
      ((Success v) (unwrap-or (json-get-string v "token") ""))
      ((Error _)   "")))

  (def-shell hole-at [w: Wv k: int] -> Json
    (unwrap-or (list-nth (w-holes w) k) json-object))

  (def-shell ptr-of [h: Json] -> string
    (unwrap-or (json-get-string h "pointer") ""))

  ;; `module-path` reads "def add-one", so the function `verify` will name in
  ;; its body-faithful list is the last space-separated field. Taking the last
  ;; and not the second is what keeps this correct for a `def-shell` hole,
  ;; whose module-path carries a different first field.
  (def-shell target-of [h: Json] -> string
    (last-field (split-on " " (unwrap-or (json-get-string h "module-path") ""))))

  ;; ---- the patch request ---------------------------------------------------

  (def-shell body-node [s: string] -> Json
    (match (json-parse s)
      ((Success v) v)
      ((Error _)   json-object)))

  ;; A ONE-OP PATCH, and the `test` op the reference sends is deliberately
  ;; absent; see the header. The CAS the compiler runs is over the brief's
  ;; source_hash and is what produces PatchAuthError, so dropping the test op
  ;; costs no contention detection.
  (def-shell patch-request [tok: string ptr: string body: string] -> string
    (json-serialize
      (jset (jset json-object "token" (json-of-string tok))
            "patch"
            (json-of-list
              [(jset (jset (jset json-object "op" (json-of-string "replace"))
                           "path" (json-of-string ptr))
                     "value" (body-node body))]))))

  ;; ---- lines ---------------------------------------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; The token half of every line is `holds-token?`, which is `token-during`.
  ;; The phase is a real argument and not a constant string, so a log line that
  ;; disagreed with the proved invariant would be a visible divergence rather
  ;; than a comment.

  (def-shell phase-line [k: int n: int what: string p: Phase] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["[wave] hole " (int-to-string k) " attempt " (int-to-string n) " " what
       " token=" (if (holds-token? p) "held" "released")]))

  (def-shell accept-line [k: int target: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["[wave] hole " (int-to-string k) " ACCEPTED " target]))

  (def-shell reject-line [k: int n: int why: string err: int proto: int] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["[wave] hole " (int-to-string k) " attempt " (int-to-string n)
       " rejected: " why
       " (error-budget " (int-to-string err)
       ", protocol-budget " (int-to-string proto) ")"]))

  ;; The classification [S9-FINDING] draws and nothing else in this module
  ;; does: an exhausted ERROR budget is a finding about the hole, and an
  ;; exhausted protocol budget is a fault of the harness that must not be
  ;; reported as one.
  (def-shell close-line [k: int fnd: bool why: string] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["[wave] hole " (int-to-string k) (if fnd " FINDING: " " PROTOCOL-FAILURE: ")
       why]))

  ;; LEADING NEWLINE, and it is not decoration. The console harness prints ONE
  ;; captured block per step, so two `wasi.io.stdout` calls inside one step's
  ;; command run together with no separator. This is the only place in the
  ;; module where that happens: `close-hole` emits its classification and then
  ;; the command `enter` returned, which on the last hole is this summary.
  (def-shell summary-line [w: Wv] -> string
    (string-concat-many
      ["\n[wave] holes " (int-to-string (list-length (w-holes w)))
       " accepted " (int-to-string (w-ok w))
       " findings " (int-to-string (w-find w))
       " protocol-failures " (int-to-string (w-bad w))]))

  ;; ---- the per-hole record -------------------------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; One row per hole, appended at the hole's terminal arm. The shape is the
  ;; reference's (stage_M_wave's `results` list) and TWO FIELDS DIVERGE, both
  ;; recorded rather than smoothed over.
  ;;
  ;; 1. STATUS. The reference writes `filled`, `finding` and `checkout-failed`.
  ;;    This module writes `filled`, `finding` and `protocol-failure`, because
  ;;    `is-finding` [S9-FINDING] draws the line at an exhausted ERROR budget and
  ;;    a checkout fault is only one of the ways the protocol budget goes. Using
  ;;    the reference's third string would report a timeout as a checkout
  ;;    failure. The comparator reads the status as a histogram KEY and does not
  ;;    match on the reference's strings, so the divergence is legible there.
  ;;
  ;; 2. ATTEMPTS IS ONE-BASED. `at-n` counts from 0 and the reference's
  ;;    `for attempt in range(1, ...)` counts from 1. R6 sums this field across
  ;;    holes, so an off-by-one here is an off-by-n in the comparator.
  ;;
  ;; `last_error` is omitted on an accepted fill, as the reference omits it.
  (def-shell fill-row [h: Json status: string attempts: int why: string] -> Json
    (let [(base (jset (jset (jset (jset json-object
                                        "hole" (json-of-string (target-of h)))
                                  "pointer" (json-of-string (ptr-of h)))
                            "status" (json-of-string status))
                      "attempts" (json-of-int attempts)))]
      (if (string-empty? why)
          base
          (jset base "last_error" (json-of-string why)))))

  (def-shell add-fill [w: Wv row: Json] -> Json
    (json-of-list (list-append (w-fills-list w) row)))

  ;; The declared output, written at the seal and nowhere else.
  ;;
  ;; `whole_tree` CARRIES TWO FIELDS AND THE REFERENCE WRITES FIVE, and the three
  ;; missing ones are missing because this module cannot measure them. `_verify`
  ;; returns `safe` (SAFE present AND returncode 0), `body_faithful` (the
  ;; fallback set parsed out of the transcript), `returncode` and `output`. The
  ;; seal arm reads the stdout FILE and never sees the exit status, so neither
  ;; `safe` nor `returncode` is available as the reference defines it. What this
  ;; module does measure is `seal-holds?`, which is SAFE present AND no
  ;; `fell back` line, and that is the conjunction of the reference's first two
  ;; minus the return code. It is written under its own name, `sealed`, because
  ;; naming it `safe` would claim a predicate this module does not evaluate.
  (def-shell wave-record [w: Wv held: bool] -> string
    (json-serialize
      (jset (jset json-object "fills" (w-fills w))
            "whole_tree"
            (jset (jset json-object
                        "file" (json-of-string (wcfg-tree (w-cfg w))))
                  "sealed" (json-of-bool held)))))

  (def-shell record-path [c: WaveCfg] -> string
    (string-concat (wcfg-workdir c) "/wave.json"))

  ;; ---- the loop ------------------------------------------------------------

  (def-shell final-code [w: Wv] -> int
    (if (> (w-find w) 0) 1 (if (> (w-bad w) 0) 3 0)))

  ;; Entry to hole k, or the close of the wave.
  ;;
  ;; THE LAST HOLE DOES NOT END THE RUN. Rev 15 measured that a fill whose body
  ;; calls an UNFILLED sibling hole clears the per-fill bar: `patch` returns
  ;; PatchSuccess, `verify` returns SAFE, and the function appears in the
  ;; body-faithful list, because that line reports the VC was EMITTED
  ;; body-faithfully and says nothing about whether its callees were assumed.
  ;; So per-fill acceptance is PROVISIONAL and the run closes on a whole-tree
  ;; `--strict-verified-core`, which is the check that can see what the
  ;; per-fill bar cannot. Q-005 records why this cannot be pushed into the
  ;; per-fill bar instead.
  (def-shell wave-enter [w: Wv k: int] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (if (>= k (list-length (w-holes w)))
        (seal w (final-code w))
        (begin-attempt w (mk-att k 0 (wcfg-err0 (w-cfg w)) (wcfg-proto0 (w-cfg w)) "" "" ""))))

  (def-shell seal [w: Wv code: int] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (let [(c (w-cfg w))]
      (wave-go w (Sealing code)
          (seq-commands
            (wasi.io.stdout (summary-line w))
            (seq-commands
              (wasi.proc.run (wcfg-compiler c)
                             ["verify" (wcfg-tree c) "--strict-verified-core"] "."
                             (string-concat (wcfg-workdir c) "/seal.out")
                             (string-concat (wcfg-workdir c) "/seal.err")
                             (wcfg-timeout c) "/dev/null")
              (wasi.fs.read (string-concat (wcfg-workdir c) "/seal.out")))))))

  ;; THE SEAL CANNOT RESCUE A RUN AND CAN ONLY CONDEMN ONE. A tally that
  ;; already reported a finding or a protocol failure keeps its code: the seal
  ;; was always going to fail on a tree with holes left in it, and reporting
  ;; that as a separate fault would count one defect twice. Code 5 is reserved
  ;; for the case the per-fill bar cannot see, every hole accepted and the tree
  ;; still not proved from its own bodies.
  ;; THE DECLARED OUTPUT IS WRITTEN HERE AND THE ORDER MATTERS. `WEnding` is not
  ;; terminal, so the command this arm returns IS performed; the command the
  ;; WEnding arm returns on the way to `WDone` is the one RC-4 drops. Writing
  ;; from the terminal arm would construct the file and never put it on disk,
  ;; which is the failure sequencer.llmll's own halt path records.
  (def-shell sealing-step [w: Wv code: int r: Response] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (let [(held (seal-holds? (wave-text-of r)))]
      (wave-go w (WEnding (if (= code 0) (if held 0 5) code))
          (seq-commands
            (wasi.fs.write (record-path (w-cfg w)) (wave-record w held))
            (wasi.io.stdout
              (string-concat-many
                ["[wave] whole-tree --strict-verified-core: "
                 (if held "SEALED" "NOT SEALED")]))))))

  ;; Step one: the directory, its declared inputs, and the brief. The token this
  ;; returns is released immediately.
  ;;
  ;; THE BRIEF IS NO LONGER THE AGENT'S ONLY INPUT, and this comment said it was
  ;; until the stage M agent contract. The provisioning below is driver-spec
  ;; section 8 part 4: where the agent needs a copy of the subject to check its
  ;; own work, the copy MUST be the original, unmodified subject. The rendered
  ;; prompt follows at `brief-step`, which is the first arm that holds the brief
  ;; text the template needs.
  ;;
  ;; THE COPIES ARE UNCHECKED, which is `ref-copies`'s tolerance in
  ;; sequencer.llmll and `if src.exists()` in the reference. `seq-commands`
  ;; performs both effects whatever the left one answered, so a missing
  ;; --reference-dir or an absent roots file leaves the agent without a file and
  ;; does not halt. Reproduced rather than hardened: a halt the reference does
  ;; not take would diverge from the oracle in a direction the comparator cannot
  ;; attribute.
  (def-shell begin-attempt [w: Wv a: Att] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (let [(c (w-cfg w))]
      (wave-go w (Brief a)
          (seq-commands
            (wasi.fs.mkdir (att-dir c a))
            (provision-cmd c a
              (seq-commands
                (llmll-run c a ["checkout" (wcfg-tree c) (ptr-of (hole-at w (at-k a)))]
                           "brief.json" "brief.err")
                (wasi.fs.read (in-dir c a "brief.json"))))))))

  ;; An empty token means the checkout itself failed (it writes prose to stderr
  ;; and leaves stdout empty, measured). That is a PROTOCOL fault and spends the
  ;; protocol budget: [S9-SEPARATE]'s reasoning applies to it for the same
  ;; reason it applies to contention, since neither says anything about whether
  ;; an agent can fill the hole.
  ;; THE PROMPT IS RENDERED HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE, because this is the first arm
  ;; that holds all four values the template needs. `r` carries the brief text,
  ;; `at-errors` carries the previous attempt's compiler output, the hole gives
  ;; the function name and the config gives the compiler command.
  ;;
  ;; THE WRITE IS SEQUENCED AHEAD OF THE RELEASE AND THE RESPONSE IS THE
  ;; RELEASE'S. `seq-commands` is discard-left, so the `Rel` arm still receives
  ;; what it expects; the write's own answer is dropped, which is what
  ;; `delegate-cmd` in sequencer.llmll does with its prompt write and what the
  ;; reference does with `prompt_path.write_text`.
  ;;
  ;; THE BRIEF REACHES THE TEMPLATE AS THE COMPILER WROTE IT. The reference
  ;; renders `json.dumps(brief, indent=1)` and this renders the raw stdout of
  ;; `llmll checkout`. Same content, different whitespace, and the clause 2
  ;; comparator reads neither.
  (def-shell brief-step [w: Wv a: Att r: Response] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (let [(c (w-cfg w))]
      (let [(tok (token-in (wave-text-of r)))]
        (if (string-empty? tok)
            ;; The previous text is CARRIED and not replaced: a checkout that
            ;; produced no token says nothing about the fill.
            (retry-or-give-up w (at-next a (at-err a) (- (at-proto a) 1) (at-errors a))
                              "the checkout produced no token")
            (let [(rendered (render-prompt (w-tpl w)
                                           (target-of (hole-at w (at-k a)))
                                           (wcfg-compiler c)
                                           (wave-text-of r)
                                           (at-errors a)))]
              (if (has-unfilled? rendered)
                  (wave-go w (WEnding 7)
                      (wasi.io.stdout
                        (string-concat-many
                          ["[wave] STOP: the rendered prompt still carries a "
                           "{{placeholder}}; the template at "
                           (wcfg-tpl-path c)
                           " names a value this wave does not substitute"])))
                  (wave-go w (Rel (at-with-token a tok))
                      (seq-commands
                        (wasi.fs.write (prompt-path c a) rendered)
                        (seq-commands
                          (wasi.io.stdout (phase-line (at-k a) (at-n a) "briefing" Briefing))
                          (llmll-run c a ["checkout" (wcfg-tree c) "--release" tok]
                                     "rel.out" "rel.err"))))))))))

  ;; THE RELEASE IS ALREADY DONE WHEN THIS ARM RUNS, so the guard is a check on
  ;; the labelling and not on the effect. `must-release-before-agent?` is
  ;; `token-during` at AgentWorking, and [S10-NOTHELD] proves it Released, so
  ;; the false branch is excluded BY THE PROOF rather than by inspection. That
  ;; is what consuming a proved decision looks like: the branch exists so that
  ;; the proof is what rules it out, and it exits 4 rather than continuing if
  ;; the invariant ever stops holding.
  ;;
  ;; THE ARGUMENT VECTOR IS SUBSTITUTED AND NOTHING IS APPENDED, which is the
  ;; stage M agent contract's item 1 and the Phase 4 proposal's section 5 item
  ;; 1 before it. This arm appended `brief.json` and `body.json` to the
  ;; operator's arguments and substituted nothing until then, so one
  ;; `--agent-arg` list served two incompatible calling conventions. MEASURED
  ;; with the 2026-09-11 campaign's real vector: the agent received `--model`,
  ;; `claude-opus-5`, the literal string `{prompt}`, the literal string `{out}`,
  ;; the brief path and the body path, and the committed wrapper tested its
  ;; third argument for readability and exited 2 before any model ran.
  ;;
  ;; A VECTOR CARRYING NO PLACEHOLDER NOW REACHES THE AGENT UNCHANGED AND EMPTY
  ;; OF PATHS. That is the contract: the operator states where the prompt and
  ;; the output go. Cover cell W1 passes `{prompt}` and `{out}` and still
  ;; asserts the agent receives exactly two paths, which is what driver-spec
  ;; section 8 is about; an argument COUNT establishes none of section 8 by
  ;; itself, and the cell's comment now says so.
  ;;
  ;; THE WORKING DIRECTORY IS THE AGENT'S OWN AND WAS ".". The template tells
  ;; the agent to run `verify scratch.llmll` and to write `body.json` in its
  ;; working directory, and both instructions need that directory to be the one
  ;; the files are in. `delegate-cmd` in sequencer.llmll and `AgentRunner.run`
  ;; in the reference both pass the agent's directory.
  (def-shell rel-step [w: Wv a: Att] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (let [(c (w-cfg w))]
      (if (must-release-before-agent? AgentWorking)
          (wave-go w (Working a)
              (seq-commands
                (wasi.io.stdout (phase-line (at-k a) (at-n a) "agent" AgentWorking))
                (seq-commands
                  (wasi.proc.run (wcfg-agent c)
                                 (list-map (wcfg-agent-args c)
                                   (fn [x: string]
                                     (subst-arg x (prompt-path c a)
                                                  (in-dir c a "body.json")
                                                  (att-dir c a))))
                                 (att-dir c a)
                                 (in-dir c a "agent.out") (in-dir c a "agent.err")
                                 (wcfg-timeout c) "/dev/null")
                  (wasi.fs.read (in-dir c a "body.json")))))
          (wave-go w (WEnding 4)
              (wasi.io.stdout
                "[wave] STOP: token-during says a token is held while an agent works")))))

  ;; No token is held on this path, so a failure retries straight into a new
  ;; brief with no release owed. An absent or empty body artifact is the
  ;; AGENT's failure and spends the ERROR budget: `spend-budget` is
  ;; `next-error-budget` with contention false, so the arithmetic is the proved
  ;; one and not a local subtraction.
  (def-shell working-step [w: Wv a: Att r: Response] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (let [(c (w-cfg w))]
      (let [(body (string-trim (wave-text-of r)))]
        (if (string-empty? body)
            ;; NEW TEXT, because this one IS about the fill. The reference sets
            ;; `errors = str(e)` on the Halt an agent timeout or a missing body
            ;; raises, and the next attempt renders it.
            (retry-or-give-up w (at-next a (spend-budget (at-err a) "") (at-proto a)
                                  "the previous attempt wrote no body.json (the agent timed out, or exited without writing its output)")
                              "the agent produced no body node")
            (wave-go w (Fresh (at-with-body a body))
                (seq-commands
                  (llmll-run c a ["checkout" (wcfg-tree c) (ptr-of (hole-at w (at-k a)))]
                             "fresh.json" "fresh.err")
                  (wasi.fs.read (in-dir c a "fresh.json"))))))))

  ;; The fresh token, taken at the moment of submission, which is [S10-FRESH].
  ;; The backup is taken here and not at boot: `patch` rewrites the tree in
  ;; place, so the undo has to restore what THIS attempt found, not what the
  ;; wave started with.
  (def-shell fresh-step [w: Wv a: Att r: Response] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (let [(c (w-cfg w))]
      (let [(tok (token-in (wave-text-of r)))]
        (if (string-empty? tok)
            ;; CARRIED, for the reason `brief-step`'s checkout failure carries
            ;; it: a checkout that produced no token says nothing about the fill.
            (retry-or-give-up w (at-next a (at-err a) (- (at-proto a) 1) (at-errors a))
                              "the resubmission checkout produced no token")
            (wave-go w (Applied (at-with-token a tok))
                (seq-commands
                  (wasi.io.stdout (phase-line (at-k a) (at-n a) "submitting" Submitting))
                  (seq-commands
                    (wasi.fs.copy (wcfg-tree c) (in-dir c a "tree.bak"))
                    (seq-commands
                      (wasi.fs.write (in-dir c a "req.json")
                                     (patch-request tok
                                                    (ptr-of (hole-at w (at-k a)))
                                                    (at-body a)))
                      (seq-commands
                        (llmll-run c a ["patch" (wcfg-tree c) (in-dir c a "req.json")]
                                   "patch.out" "patch.err")
                        (wasi.fs.read (in-dir c a "patch.out")))))))))))

  ;; THE TWO BUDGETS PART HERE, and the arithmetic is `next-error-budget`'s,
  ;; not this module's: `spend-budget` passes `contention? out`, so [S9-SEPARATE]
  ;; leaves the error budget alone on contention and [S9-SPEND] takes one from
  ;; it on anything else. The protocol budget moves in the mirror direction.
  ;;
  ;; `contention?` is narrower than PatchAuthError on purpose: `invalid or
  ;; expired checkout token` carries the same constructor and is NOT contention.
  ;;
  ;; WHERE THE CONTENTION BRANCH IS REACHABLE FROM, since a serial wave never
  ;; holds two of its own briefs at once. The CAS is over the brief's
  ;; source_hash, so the window is the ONE console step between the fresh
  ;; checkout issued in `working-step` and the patch issued in `fresh-step`: a
  ;; tree that changes inside it makes this attempt's brief stale. The console
  ;; loop takes one line of stdin per step, so a cover cell drives that window
  ;; by feeding a line, patching the tree from outside, then feeding the next.
  ;; Rev 15's F-27 is what this rests on -- contention needs a second brief
  ;; outstanding across a successful patch and not a stub -- and the two-brief
  ;; cover cell it mandates is owed, not written.
  (def-shell applied-step [w: Wv a: Att r: Response] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (let [(c (w-cfg w))]
      (let [(out (wave-text-of r))]
        (if (patch-succeeded? out)
            (wave-go w (Checked a)
                (seq-commands
                  (llmll-run c a ["verify" (wcfg-tree c)] "verify.out" "verify.err")
                  (wasi.fs.read (in-dir c a "verify.out"))))
            (undo w a
                  (spend-budget (at-err a) out)
                  (if (contention? out) (- (at-proto a) 1) (at-proto a))
                  (if (contention? out) "contention" "the patch was refused")
                  out)))))

  ;; The per-fill bar, section 9, and `applied` is `true` by construction here:
  ;; this arm is reachable only through `patch-succeeded?`. The other three
  ;; conjuncts are the shell-side observations at the top of this file, and
  ;; `fill-accepted` is what combines them.
  ;;
  ;; THE RELEASE ON THE ACCEPT PATH IS A NO-OP AND IS ISSUED ANYWAY. Measured:
  ;; a successful `patch` clears the lock itself, so this release answers
  ;; `token not found in lock file (may have expired)` on stderr and nothing on
  ;; stdout. It stays because the invariant this module keeps is that no token
  ;; outlives its hole, and making that depend on an undocumented side effect
  ;; of `patch` is how the wedge in crux-token-held-across-call happened. The
  ;; same call on the `undo` path is NOT a no-op: a patch that was refused
  ;; leaves the lock held, and that is the case it exists for.
  (def-shell checked-step [w: Wv a: Att r: Response] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (let [(c (w-cfg w))]
      (let [(out (wave-text-of r))]
        (let [(target (target-of (hole-at w (at-k a))))]
          (if (accept-fill? true out target)
              (wave-go w (Closing a)
                  (seq-commands
                    (wasi.io.stdout (accept-line (at-k a) target))
                    (llmll-run c a ["checkout" (wcfg-tree c) "--release" (at-token a)]
                               "relok.out" "relok.err")))
              (undo w a (spend-budget (at-err a) out) (at-proto a)
                    "the fill did not clear the per-fill bar" out))))))

  ;; Restore, then release. IN THAT ORDER and both are owed: the restore undoes
  ;; a patch that may have landed, and the release is what keeps the next
  ;; checkout from failing with `already checked out`. Skipping the release
  ;; wedges the hole, measured.
  ;; `why` AND `errs` ARE TWO STRINGS AND NOT ONE. `why` is the short reason the
  ;; reject line prints and the fill row records; `errs` is the compiler's whole
  ;; transcript, which the next attempt's prompt renders as {{errors}}. Folding
  ;; them would either put a 2 KB transcript in a log line or hand the agent
  ;; four words where the reference hands it the compiler's output.
  (def-shell undo [w: Wv a: Att err: int proto: int why: string errs: string]
      -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (let [(c (w-cfg w))]
      (wave-go w (Undoing (at-next a err proto errs))
          (seq-commands
            (wasi.io.stdout (reject-line (at-k a) (at-n a) why err proto))
            (seq-commands
              (wasi.fs.copy (in-dir c a "tree.bak") (wcfg-tree c))
              (llmll-run c a ["checkout" (wcfg-tree c) "--release" (at-token a)]
                         "relundo.out" "relundo.err"))))))

  ;; `a` arrives already advanced by at-next, so its budgets are the ones the
  ;; decision below is about.
  (def-shell retry-or-give-up [w: Wv a: Att why: string] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (if (and (> (at-err a) 0) (> (at-proto a) 0))
        (begin-attempt w a)
        (close-hole w a why)))

  ;; `is-finding` and nothing local decides which counter moves. Only an
  ;; exhausted ERROR budget is a finding [S9-FINDING]; a hole that ran out of
  ;; protocol budget while its error budget still had room is NOT one
  ;; [S9-NOT-FINDING], and reporting it as one would blame the agent for a
  ;; harness fault. The clamp is because `is-finding` carries `pre (>= err 0)`.
  (def-shell close-hole [w: Wv a: Att why: string] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (let [(fnd (finding? (clamp0 (at-err a)) (<= (at-proto a) 0)))]
      ;; `at-n` AND NOT `at-n + 1`, which is the opposite of the accepted path
      ;; above, and the two are counting different things. `at-next` bumps `n`
      ;; when an attempt has FINISHED and a retry is about to be considered, so
      ;; the attempt this arm sees is the one that never ran: at a budget of 2
      ;; the hole is closed at n=2 after exactly two attempts. `closing-step`
      ;; instead names the attempt that SUCCEEDED, which is an index, so it adds
      ;; one. Measured by cover cell W9, which rejected `at-n + 1` here.
      ;;
      ;; This also matches the reference, whose exhausted row carries
      ;; `ctx.semantic_retries` rather than a loop counter.
      (let [(fl (add-fill w (fill-row (hole-at w (at-k a))
                                      (if fnd "finding" "protocol-failure")
                                      (at-n a) why)))]
        (let [(w2 (if fnd
                      (mk-wv (w-cfg w) (w-holes w) (w-ok w) (+ (w-find w) 1) (w-bad w) fl (w-tpl w))
                      (mk-wv (w-cfg w) (w-holes w) (w-ok w) (w-find w) (+ (w-bad w) 1) fl (w-tpl w))))]
          (let [(e (wave-enter w2 (+ (at-k a) 1)))]
            (pair (first e)
                  (seq-commands (wasi.io.stdout (close-line (at-k a) fnd why))
                                (second e))))))))

  (def-shell undoing-step [w: Wv a: Att] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (retry-or-give-up w a "every attempt at this hole was rejected"))

  (def-shell closing-step [w: Wv a: Att] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (wave-enter (mk-wv (w-cfg w) (w-holes w) (+ (w-ok w) 1) (w-find w) (w-bad w)
                       (add-fill w (fill-row (hole-at w (at-k a)) "filled"
                                             (+ (at-n a) 1) ""))
                       (w-tpl w))
           (+ (at-k a) 1)))

  ;; ---- boot ----------------------------------------------------------------

  ;; THE SEQUENCER'S ENTRY, past the argv parse. Program unification job (a2)
  ;; folds stage M into the stage loop, and the sequencer arrives here with a
  ;; `WaveCfg` it built from its own `Cfg` and the registry rather than from a
  ;; command line. Splitting the parse off is what lets one machine serve both
  ;; the stage arm and the standalone `wave` sub-command the 4e cover drives.
  ;; THE TEMPLATE IS READ ONCE AND RENDERED MANY TIMES, and the split matters.
  ;; The template is static and the values are per attempt, so a read per
  ;; attempt would buy nothing and would move a `--prompts-dir` fault from boot
  ;; into the middle of a run. `WBoot 2` is the read and `WBoot 1` is the hole
  ;; list, which is the order they are needed in.
  (def-shell wave-seed [c: WaveCfg] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (wave-go (mk-wv c (list-empty) 0 0 0 (json-of-list (list-empty)) "") (WBoot 2)
        (wasi.fs.read (wcfg-tpl-path c))))

  (def-shell wave-holes-cmd [c: WaveCfg] -> Command
    (seq-commands
      (wasi.fs.mkdir (wcfg-workdir c))
      (seq-commands
        (wasi.proc.run (wcfg-compiler c) ["holes" (wcfg-tree c) "--json"] "."
                       (string-concat (wcfg-workdir c) "/holes.json")
                       (string-concat (wcfg-workdir c) "/holes.err")
                       (wcfg-timeout c) "/dev/null")
        (wasi.fs.read (string-concat (wcfg-workdir c) "/holes.json")))))

  ;; AN EMPTY READ AND AN ABSENT FILE TAKE THE SAME ARM, and both halt. A
  ;; missing template answers RErr, which `wave-text-of` maps to "", and a
  ;; present but empty template answers "" directly. Both hand the agent no task
  ;; statement, so one code and a message naming both causes is right here; the
  ;; same shape `fan-join` uses for code 2, which also carries two causes.
  ;; Code 6 and not 2: 2 is the usage stop and the empty tree, and the stage arm
  ;; must be able to say which of the three happened.
  (def-shell wave-tmpl-step [w: Wv r: Response] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (let [(c (w-cfg w))]
      (let [(tpl (wave-text-of r))]
        (if (string-empty? tpl)
            (wave-go w (WEnding 6)
                (wasi.io.stdout
                  (string-concat-many
                    ["[wave] STOP: the prompt template at " (wcfg-tpl-path c)
                     " is not readable, or is empty. The rendered task statement "
                     "is a declared input (driver-spec sec 8), so the wave does "
                     "not run without it."])))
            (wave-go (w-with-tpl w tpl) (WBoot 1) (wave-holes-cmd c))))))

  ;; THE ARGV PATH, which the `wave` sub-command takes and the stage arm does
  ;; not. A LEADING "wave" TOKEN IS HARMLESS HERE and is deliberately not
  ;; stripped: `flag-value` and `flag-values` scan for an element EQUAL to the
  ;; flag name and take the one after it, so an element that is not a flag name
  ;; is skipped. Dropping it would need a list operation this tree does not have.
  (def-shell wave-boot-args [as: list[string]] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (let [(c (parse-wave-cfg as))]
      (if (string-empty? (wave-missing-flags c))
          (wave-seed c)
          ;; Before any hole exists, so no tally attaches. The literal 2 is
          ;; sequencer.llmll's usage-stop code.
          (wave-go (mk-wv c (list-empty) 0 0 0 (json-of-list (list-empty)) "") (WEnding 2)
              (wasi.io.stdout (string-concat "[wave] STOP: " (wave-missing-flags c)))))))

  (def-shell wave-boot-step [w: Wv n: int r: Response] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (if (= n 0)
        (wave-boot-args (argv-of r))
      (if (= n 2)
        (wave-tmpl-step w r)
        (let [(hs (holes-in (wave-text-of r)))]
          (if (= (list-length hs) 0)
              ;; A tree with nothing to fill. Not the .llmll mistake, which
              ;; `missing-flags` now stops at parse: `holes` answers the real
              ;; list for a .llmll too, so this arm only ever sees a genuinely
              ;; hole-free tree or a `holes` invocation that failed.
              (wave-go w (WEnding 2)
                  (wasi.io.stdout
                    (string-concat-many
                      ["[wave] STOP: no holes in " (wcfg-tree (w-cfg w))
                       " (nothing to fill, or `llmll holes` did not run; its "
                       "stderr is in the workdir as holes.err)"])))
              (wave-enter (mk-wv (w-cfg w) hs 0 0 0 (json-of-list (list-empty)) (w-tpl w)) 0))))))

  ;; ---- the console harness -------------------------------------------------
  ;;
  ;; NO def-main. Program unification job (a2) folded stage M into
  ;; `sequencer.llmll`'s stage loop, so this module is a LIBRARY and the driver
  ;; is one program. The machine below is unchanged; what moved is who drives
  ;; it. The sequencer's `Fan` arm carries a `(Wv, WCtl)` and hands each step to
  ;; `wave-step`, and the `wave` sub-command runs the same machine with the
  ;; stage index -1, which is how the seven 4e cells keep grading it.

  (def-shell wave-step [s: (Wv, WCtl) input: string r: Response] -> ((Wv, WCtl), Command)
    (match (ctl-of s)
      ((WBoot n)    (wave-boot-step    (w-of s) n r))
      ((Brief a)   (brief-step   (w-of s) a r))
      ((Rel a)     (rel-step     (w-of s) a))
      ((Working a) (working-step (w-of s) a r))
      ((Fresh a)   (fresh-step   (w-of s) a r))
      ((Applied a) (applied-step (w-of s) a r))
      ((Checked a) (checked-step (w-of s) a r))
      ((Closing a) (closing-step (w-of s) a))
      ((Undoing a) (undoing-step (w-of s) a))
      ((Sealing c) (sealing-step (w-of s) c r))
      ((WEnding c)  (wave-go (w-of s) (WDone c) (wasi.io.stdout "")))
      ((WDone c)    (wave-go (w-of s) (WDone c) (wasi.io.stdout "")))))

  (def-shell wave-done? [s: (Wv, WCtl)] -> bool
    (match (ctl-of s)
      ((WBoot _)    false)
      ((Brief _)   false)
      ((Rel _)     false)
      ((Working _) false)
      ((Fresh _)   false)
      ((Applied _) false)
      ((Checked _) false)
      ((Closing _) false)
      ((Undoing _) false)
      ((Sealing _) false)
      ((WEnding _)  false)
      ((WDone _)    true)))

  (def-shell wave-done-code [s: (Wv, WCtl)] -> int
    (match (ctl-of s)
      ((WBoot _)    1)
      ((Brief _)   1)
      ((Rel _)     1)
      ((Working _) 1)
      ((Fresh _)   1)
      ((Applied _) 1)
      ((Checked _) 1)
      ((Closing _) 1)
      ((Undoing _) 1)
      ((Sealing _) 1)
      ((WEnding _)  1)
      ((WDone c)    c)))

  ;; THE CODE AT AN ENDING OR TERMINAL ARM, and a negative at every other. This
  ;; is what the sequencer's stage M arm reads, and `wave-done-code` cannot
  ;; serve: that one answers only at `WDone`, which is one step too late. The
  ;; `WEnding` step is what the fold must intercept, because a folded stage M
  ;; hands the run back to the stage loop instead of reaching a program exit.
  ;;
  ;; NEGATIVE AND NOT 0 for the "still running" answer, because 0 is the success
  ;; code and the two must not be the same value.
  ;;
  ;; Kept beside the two matches above so a new `WCtl` arm has to be answered in
  ;; three places at once; `test_driver_ll_4e.py` asserts all three agree.
  (def-shell wave-exit-code [s: (Wv, WCtl)] -> int
    (match (ctl-of s)
      ((WBoot _)   (- 0 1))
      ((Brief _)   (- 0 1))
      ((Rel _)     (- 0 1))
      ((Working _) (- 0 1))
      ((Fresh _)   (- 0 1))
      ((Applied _) (- 0 1))
      ((Checked _) (- 0 1))
      ((Closing _) (- 0 1))
      ((Undoing _) (- 0 1))
      ((Sealing _) (- 0 1))
      ((WEnding c)  c)
      ((WDone c)    c)))

  ;; `wave-final` IS GONE, and the one line it printed is gone with it. It was
  ;; this module's `:on-done`, and there is no `def-main` here to carry one. The
  ;; unified program's `drv-final` prints `[driver-ll] end status=N` for every
  ;; run including a `wave` sub-command run, which is true of what now runs. No
  ;; cover cell reads that line; the seven 4e cells read the exit code and the
  ;; per-hole transcript.
  ;;
  ;; def-shell and NOT a proved def, unlike sequencer.llmll's drv-status. That
  ;; one carries [EXIT-RANGE] because PROC-BOUNDARY-1 section 4.4 declares a
  ;; range for the driver's exit code; no clause declares one for the wave, and
  ;; writing a post here would be inventing a contract rather than porting one.
  ;; The clamp still fails in the safe direction: 256 exits 255, not 0.
  ;;
  ;; THE CLAMP IS WHAT KEEPS `drv-status`'s DECLARED RANGE TRUE. The sequencer's
  ;; `:status` carries `post (and (>= result 0) (<= result 255))` and its stage M
  ;; arm returns this function's answer, so an arm that read `wave-done-code`
  ;; directly would let a code of 256 exit 0 and report success on a failed run.
  (def-shell wave-status [s: (Wv, WCtl)] -> int
    (let [(c (wave-done-code s))]
      (if (< c 0) 1 (if (> c 255) 255 c)))))
